[07:39.68]Passage One
[07:41.60]Annually, obesity accounts for about $200 billion, or about 20%, of our national health care costs. 
[07:51.94]It costs billions more in sick leaves and lost productivity. 
[07:57.07]Over 80% of people who lose 10% or more of their weight will regain it, and for all the new treatments, that number hasn't really improved in a generation.
[08:10.07]Though reluctantly, many researchers admit that there is no long-term miracle weight loss pill. 
[08:16.62]We should seek hope by investing in new ideas and more personalized approaches to combating the obesity epidemic.
[08:25.86]One way is to focus on obesity prevention, which starts in the home, school and community. 
[08:33.51]Numerous studies suggest that the behavior we learn early in life influences our behavior as adults. 
[08:41.36]We can potentially delay or even prevent adult diseases by fostering better health habits in our children.
[08:49.60]Schools are an excellent place in which to orient families toward healthy behavior. 
[08:55.66]Teachers, in addition to providing health and physical education, can serve as role models and engage parents in good health practices for their children.
[09:06.11]Health education is a learning process, and, like any academic area, it needs to be developed over time. 
[09:14.84]Good nutrition and exercise habits are good for all of us¡ªfat or thin¡ªand most of us could use a refresher course.
[09:24.29]There is hope, but we have to use common sense and good science to make sure we're on the right path. 
[09:31.87]As Charles Darwin put it, ¡°the more we know of the fixed laws of nature the more incredible do miracles become.¡±
[09:41.92]Questions 9 to 11 are based on the passage you have just heard.
[09:48.07]9. What do many researchers admit concerning weight loss?
[10:09.04]10. What do numerous studies reveal according the speaker?
[10:29.01]11. What does the speaker say about health education?
[10:48.94]Passage Two
[10:51.98]Researchers have found significant variations in the length, timing, and quality of people's sleep. 
[10:58.91]For instance, children and adolescents need much more sleep than do adults. 
[11:04.93]Women tend to have more insomnia than men. 
[11:08.19]Some people do well with 6 hours of sleep a night, others need at least 8 or 9. 
[11:15.26]Some people are most alert early in the morning, while others are slow to awaken and only feel truly conscious late at night. 
[11:24.07]All of this is to say, there is no right or normal way of sleeping. 
[11:28.65]There is only what makes you and your body feel rested.
[11:32.37]These two basic findings of sleep science¡ªthe necessity and variability of sleep¡ªhave important implications for how work and school activities are scheduled. 
[11:43.03]For example, it's not healthy for schools or businesses to establish a single schedule governing the activities of a large group of people without building in flexibility for the significant number of people whose bodily rhythms will need a somewhat different schedule to stay optimally functional.
[12:01.22]It often takes time for scientific research to overcome entrenched cultural attitudes. 
[12:07.03]A view of sleep as nothing but wasted time has prevailed among many people. 
[12:11.95]Denying the need for sleep has become a symbol of heroic devotion to productivity, innovation, and success. 
[12:19.55]Anyone who believes otherwise is simply weak or lazy, a slacker with no drive.
[12:25.53]That attitude should be left behind. 
[12:28.31]The truth is that, given the enormous challenges facing society today, people with sound sleep patterns are more creative, better able to respond adaptively to unusual or unexpected circumstances as compared to sleep-deprived people.
[12:46.35]Questions 12 to 15 are based on the passage you have just heard.
[12:52.13]12. What have researchers found about sleep?
[13:10.02]13. What does the finding about sleep suggest?
[13:28.55]14. What are the two basic findings of sleep science?
[13:47.48]15. What is the popular view concerning sleep?
