[14:35.55]Recording One
[14:37.56]Astronomers have long understood that the universe is expanding. 
[14:42.43]Then, in 1998, scientists discovered the universe's rate of expansion is accelerating.
[14:50.41]In the years since, researchers using the Hubble Space Telescope have observed that the current expansion rate, called the Hubble constant, is faster than what standard models estimate it should be. 
[15:06.38]This mystery of inconsistent measurements has been called the Hubble tension.
[15:11.99]Now, a team of researchers has used data from the James Webb Space Telescope to confirm Hubble's measurements¡ªand thus, the mystery persists. 
[15:23.46]Their study suggests the Hubble tension may be caused by an unknown feature in the universe, rather than an error in telescope measurement. 
[15:33.72]The finding suggests that our understanding of the universe may be incomplete. 
[15:39.36]¡°With two NASA flagship telescopes now confirming each other's findings, we must take this Hubble tension problem very seriously¡ªit's a challenge but also an incredible opportunity to learn more about our universe,¡± said Adam Riess, an astrophysicist and lead author of the study. 
[16:01.17]He says that it appears there is something missing in our understanding of the universe. 
[16:07.10]Our understanding of the universe contains a lot of ignorance about two elements¡ªdark matter and dark energy¡ªand these make up 96 percent of the universe, so this is no small matter. 
[16:22.16]Dark matter, hypothesized to make up 27 percent of the universe, is some unknown material that affects the rest of the universe with its gravity. 
[16:33.36]Dark energy, on the other hand, is thought to make up some 68 percent of the universe, and scientists feel it might be responsible for the universe's expansion. 
[16:45.32]And now, the new findings suggest it perhaps accounts for the unexplained acceleration of that expansion, too.
[16:53.76]¡°The more work we do, the more it is apparent that the cause is something much more interesting than a telescope flaw,¡± Riess says.
[17:04.07]¡°One possible explanation for the Hubble tension would be if there was something missing in our understanding of the early universe, such as a new component of matter¡ªearly dark energy¡ªthat gave the universe an unexpected kick after the Big Bang,¡± a cosmologist says in a statement. 
[17:25.33]¡°And there are other ideas, like funny dark matter properties, exotic particles, changing electron mass or early-stage magnetic fields that may do the trick.Theorists have license to get pretty creative.¡±
[17:44.37]Questions 16 to 18 are based on the recording you have just heard.
[17:49.56]16. What have scientists found in 1998?
[18:07.82]17. What is likely to be the cause of Hubble tension according to researchers?
[18:27.10]18. What can we learn about dark matter?
[18:45.63]Recording Two
[18:47.48]Drinking a few cups of coffee a day may help people avoid clogged arteries¡ªa known risk factor for heart disease¡ªKorean researchers believe.
[18:55.66]They studied more than 25,000 male and female employees who underwent routine health checks.
[19:01.64]Employees who drank three to five cups a day were less likely to have early signs of heart disease on their medical scans.
[19:08.84]The findings reopen the debate about whether coffee is good for the heart.
[19:13.13]There is a lot of confusion when it comes to the effect of coffee on heart health.
[19:17.83]Some studies have linked consumption to heart risk factors, such as raised cholesterol or blood pressure, while others suggest the beverage may offer some heart protection.
[19:27.61]But there is no conclusive evidence either way, and the latest research published in the journal Heart only adds to the discussion.
[19:35.67]In the study, the researchers used medical scans to assess heart health. 
[19:40.28]Specifically, they were looking for any disease of the arteries supplying the heart¡ªthe coronary arteries.
[19:46.71]In coronary heart disease, the coronary arteries become clogged by the gradual build-up of fatty material within their walls.
[19:54.37]The scan the researchers used looks for tiny deposits of calcium in the walls of the coronary arteries to provide an early clue that this disease process may be occurring.
[20:04.52]None of the employees included in the Korean study had outward signs of heart disease, but more than one in ten of them were found to have visible calcium deposits on their scans.
[20:14.92]The researchers then compared the scan results with the employees' self-reported daily coffee consumption, while taking into account other potential heart risk factors such as smoking and family history of heart problems.
[20:27.86]People who drank a few cups of coffee a day were less likely to have calcium deposits in their coronary arteries than people who drank more than this or no coffee at all.
[20:37.67]The study authors say more research is needed to confirm and explain the link. 
[20:43.02]Coffee contains the stimulant caffeine, as well as numerous other compounds, but it's not clear if these might cause good or harm to the body.
[20:52.97]Questions 19 to 21 are based on the recording you have just heard.
[20:58.90]19. Which of the following is in accord with Korean researchers' belief?
[21:18.83]20. What were the researchers specifically looking for when assessing heart health?
[21:38.65]21. What other potential heart risk factor has been taken into account in the study?
[21:59.98]Recording Three
[22:02.32]Picture this: You want to apply for a dream assignment abroad. 
[22:07.60]There's just one problem. 
[22:09.23]You need foreign language skills¡ªand time is not on your side.
[22:14.45]It might sound like an impossible task, but according to language experts, you can learn basic communication skills in weeks and master the basics of a foreign language in several months.
[22:26.70]Sometimes traveling the globe for work will force you to come up with ways to master conversation in many languages. 
[22:33.84]Benny Lewis, an engineer, learned enough of seven languages¡ªincluding Spanish, French and German¡ªto work easily.
[22:43.77]Learning Spanish, Lewis's first non-native language, took over a year but subsequent languages were quicker.
[22:52.20]Phrasebooks and online tutorials can prove useful in this early stage, experts say, as they can give you the vocabulary and the confidence to have basic conversations with native speakers, the crucial first step in learning a language.
[23:08.38]Indeed, simply having the courage to speak is necessary if you are going to make progress in a foreign language, language experts say. 
[23:17.70]This means not being afraid to take risks or make mistakes.
[23:21.71]Experts believe that total immersion is key to mastering a foreign language quickly. 
[23:27.78]The more you immerse yourself in the foreign language the more rapid your progress will be.
[23:33.53]Getting to basic conversational proficiency can be achieved in much less time, just several weeks according to experts, particularly if you can speak regularly. 
[23:44.54]James North, associate director for instruction at the Foreign Service Institute, said students are encouraged to get to know native speakers.
[23:53.89]By conversing regularly with language experts or native speakers you also have someone to check and correct your progress.
[24:02.13]You'll need to ask those you speak with for feedback and make sure they can correct your pronunciation and grammar, although experts say you need not worry too much about grammar in the early stages.
[24:14.71]Use the language first and focus on the grammar later, Lewis said. 
[24:19.69]When you are ready to pick up the grammar, he recommended using podcasts at sites such as radiolingua.com.
[24:27.89]As you learn, be sure to consume media in the foreign language. 
[24:32.50]If you are starting out, read illustrated children's books in a foreign language, experts advise.
[24:38.97]But beware of lofty ambitions. 
[24:41.73]If you say you want to be fluent in two months, you will likely be disappointed. 
[24:46.92]But if your goal is to reach some level of conversational proficiency, that's entirely possible.
